# THE LEDGER IS DURABLE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT.
#
# Every other example in this corpus binds its aggregates to `Memory`
# because it is a corpus member, exercised by a spec and thrown away. This
# one is not: `qa/FINDINGS.md` is a file that survives the session, and a
# replacement for it that lived in a process would be strictly worse than
# the markdown it replaces.
#
# HEKI RATHER THAN POSTGRES, deliberately. Heki is a file per aggregate
# under `qa/data/` with a journal beside it — it needs no server, no
# credentials and no network, so `bin/quality_control` and the MCP server
# both work on a laptop with nothing running. It is also NOT lineage-capable
# (`Adapters::Postgres` is the only adapter that declares
# `lineage_capable?`), which means `EraCheck` does not gate a boot of this
# domain: editing the bluebook mid-practice does not demand an era be minted
# before the ledger can be opened again. That is the right trade for a tool
# whose own shape is still moving.
#
# NO PROJECTION. `banking.hecksagon` pairs every `persisted_by` with a
# `projected_by("SqliteProjection")`; this does not, because nothing here
# reads across aggregates at a volume a projection would help — the largest
# collection this ledger will ever hold is a few hundred bugs, and every
# query in the chapter filters on a lifecycle state the head already
# carries.
Hecks.hecksagon "QualityControl" do
  QualityControl::Session.persisted_by("Heki")
  QualityControl::Bug.persisted_by("Heki")
  QualityControl::Remedy.persisted_by("Heki")
  QualityControl::Ticket.persisted_by("Heki")
  QualityControl::Target.persisted_by("Heki")
end
